❓Question:
Given an integer array nums and a value val, remove all occurrences of val in-place and return the number of elements that are not equal to val.

For example:
Input:  nums = [3,2,2,3], val = 3
Output: 2
The first k = 2 elements of the array should contain:
[2,2]
The remaining elements don't matter.

✅Approach:
Use a variable k to represent the position where the next valid element should be placed. Traverse the array, and whenever the current element is not equal to val, copy it to nums[k] and increment k.

Code:
class Solution {
    public int removeElement(int[] nums, int val) {
        int k = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (nums[i] != val) {
                nums[k] = nums[i];
                k++;
            }
        }
        return k;
    }
}

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)
